{"abstract":"Digital security in all its forms, whether focused on computer security, information security, or cyber security, has practices of modelling at its core. Over recent decades, the goals of security in a digital context have widened to cover not only the protection of data and computing resource but also the protection and enablement of people using technology. Alongside this, there has been a concomitant diversification of the types of modelling that are used in digital security. In recent years, modellers have faced significant epistemological challenges, with tensions between different interdisciplinary perspectives about what models and modelling ought to be, and difficulties adequately communicating the virtues of different kinds of models among modellers and stakeholders such as policymakers or users. In this paper, we present a framework grounded in philosophy and social theory for understanding the methodological diversity of security modelling today: the MORS grid. Using the MORS grid, modellers can locate their own work of modelling, and explore methodological variations and political implications. We argue that in a subject that is increasingly recognized as being composed of different disciplinary positions, such a grid not only enables modellers to locate their own approach but also to appreciate the modelling positions of others. The grid is composed of four simple binaries: whether the modeller is an expert or non-expert, whether the modelling enquiry is model-oriented or target-oriented, whether the referent object (what needs to be protected) is determined prior to or posterior to the task of modelling, and whether the analyst adopts the design stance or the intentional stance. The paper presents the MORS grid through three lines of thought: first, we unpack the theoretical basis for each distinction in existing literature in the philosophy of science, security studies, and philosophy of mind; second, we provide a historical review of security modelling, and examine which positions on the MORS grid have predominated, and why; third, we set out the implications for modellers, policymakers and other stakeholders.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Spencer, Matt","Coles-Kemp, Lizzie","Hansen, Rene Rydhof"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-770050"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Spencer, Matt"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Royal Holloway University of London","University of Warwick"],"keyword":["Intentional stance","Modelling","Participatory Design","security models"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"navigating the landscape of security modelling the mors grid","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/cybsec/tyae024","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Matt Spencer acknowledges support for this research from UK Research and Innovation, grant number MR/S037373/1."],"id":"S01-000.00-770050","identifier":[{"id":"85209695072","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-35032fc0-1ce0-4688-9d1a-c24fc207ed25"],"keyword":["intentional stance","modelling","participatory design","security models","socio-technical security"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-45114ED305362709FB22E3DA0C381F48"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/757786746/tyae024.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/cybsec/tyae024","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of warwick","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Warwick","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"royal holloway university of london","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Royal Holloway University of London","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science, Distributed, Embedded and Intelligent Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Matt","key":"spencerpau","last":"Spencer","name":"Spencer, Matt","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lizzie","key":"coleskemppau","last":"Coles-Kemp","name":"Coles-Kemp, Lizzie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rene Rydhof","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"d92ec90d-a8b7-4c01-acbe-ebab21f3f0c3","name":"Hansen, Rene Rydhof","orcid":"0000-0002-5688-6432","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/cybsec/tyae024","issn":["20572093"],"issue":"1","journal":"Journal of Cybersecurity","paperid":"tyae024","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"Journal of Cybersecurity","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85209695072"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-35032fc0-1ce0-4688-9d1a-c24fc207ed25"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Navigating the landscape of security modelling : the MORS grid","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-770050","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Knowledge of how to treat chronic tendinopathy has advanced in recent years, but the treatment of early tendinopathy is not well understood. The main purpose of this prospective observational study was to investigate if changes occur in clinical and imaging outcomes over 12 weeks in elite athletes with recent debut of tendinopathy. Sixty-five elite adult athletes (24 ± 5 years) with early Achilles or patellar tendinopathy (symptoms < 3 months) were examined at baseline and after 12 weeks. Patients were divided into groups based on the duration of symptoms at the time of inclusion: (T1): 0-1 month, (T2): 1-2 months, or (T3): 2-3 months. Pain-guided activity modification was the only intervention. We assessed the following clinical outcomes: Questionnaires (Victorian Institute of Sports Assessment (VISA)) and pain scores (0-10 numeric rating scale, NRS), structural outcomes from ultrasonography: Thickness, echogenicity, and Doppler flow, and from magnetic resonance imaging: Cross-sectional area (CSA), thickness and length. Tendinopathic Achilles and patellar tendons displayed no significant differences on imaging tendon structural outcomes between T1 (n = 19), T2 (n = 23), and T3 (n = 20) at baseline or after 12 weeks, with one exception: Patellar tendons in T1 were thicker than T2 and T3 at baseline. Although athletes improved clinically on VISA and most NRS scores after 12 weeks, affected tendons with greater thickness, CSA and Doppler flow than contralateral tendons at baseline remained unchanged after 12 weeks. In conclusion, these data suggest that early tendinopathy in elite athletes can improve clinically after 12 weeks while morphology remains unchanged.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Meulengracht, Christopher Strandberg","Seidler, Marc","Svensson, Rene Brüggebusch","Kracht, Mathilde","Zeidan, Ahmad","Christensen, Kasper Ørnsvig","Jensen, Mikkel Holm Hjortshøj","Hansen, Phillip","Boesen, Mikael","Brushøj, Christoffer","Magnusson, Stig Peter","Bahr, Roald","Kjær, Michael","Couppé, Christian"],"cuid":["S02-099.70-769198"],"filters":{"added":"2024-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Norwegian School of Sport Sciences"],"keyword":["Achilles","Achilles Tendon diagnostic imaging","Adult","Athletes","Elite","Female","Humans","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","Male","Pain Measurement","Patellar","Patellar Ligament diagnostic imaging","Prospective Studies","Surveys and Questionnaires","Tendinopathy","Tendinopathy diagnostic imaging","Ultrasonography","Ultrasound","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Research Unit of Clinical Nursing, Bispebjerg and Frederiksberg University Hospital"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"],"UC":["DI-UC-AB","DI-UC-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-UC-AB"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"clinical and imaging outcomes over 000000000012 weeks in elite athletes with early stage tendinopathy","src":["ku","pha"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-UC-AB"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/sms.14732","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.70-769198","identifier":[{"id":"39358891","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85205527926","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-c0b8c695-c778-4ad3-9301-1d51e9b15cdc","pha-3f900ba9-ab25-432d-aad4-aa546f1cceca"],"keyword":["Achilles","Achilles Tendon/diagnostic imaging","Adult","Athletes","Female","Humans","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","Male","Pain Measurement","Patellar Ligament/diagnostic imaging","Prospective Studies","Surveys and Questionnaires","Tendinopathy/diagnostic imaging","Ultrasonography","Young Adult","elite","patellar","tendinitis","tendinopathy","ultrasound"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-BDE6CBC63C2F3650E4CC007E4B34AF53"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/sms.14732","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/410082510/Scandinavian_Med_Sci_Sports_2024_Meulengracht_Clinical_and_Imaging_Outcomes_Over_12_Weeks_in_Elite_Athletes_With.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Bispebjerg and Frederiksberg"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Research Unit of Clinical Nursing, Bispebjerg and Frederiksberg University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"university college absalon","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-AB","l3nl":"University College Absalon","l3nn":"University College Absalon","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University College Absalon","namevar":["Professionshøjskolen Absalon, Direktionen - Slagelse, Sundhed, Social og Engineeringområdet, Absalon - Rehabilitering og bevægelse","Professionshøjs