{"abstract":"Aim: As survival of infants born prematurely has increased dramatically, questions on long-term consequences have emerged. Our aim was to investigate long-term effects of very low birth weight on socioeconomic outcomes. Methods: One hundred and fifty very low birth weight infants (VLBW) born from 1980 to 1982 at Rigshospitalet, Denmark, who had previously been followed up at age 2, 4 and 18 years, were compared to cohorts of low birth weight, normal birth weight (NBW) and a national population-based reference cohort. From the Danish national registers we obtained data regarding educational level, financial independence and living arrangements. In addition, we used the previously published results from the three cohorts. Results: The VLBW cohort had lower intelligence quotient and higher risk of significant school difficulties evaluated at age 4 and 18 years. When compared to the NBW cohort, at 30–36 years of age the VLBW cohort tended to have lower educational level, OR 1.7, 95% CI 0.8–3.9, were not financially independent OR 1.5, 95% CI 0.6–3.7, lived alone OR 2.0, 95% CI 1.0–3.8 and had higher rates of the combination of all three outcomes, OR 3.2, 95% CI 0.7–15.8. Conclusions: We found trends towards poor socioeconomic outcomes in young adults born with VLBW. The relative disadvantages appeared smaller than that in childhood.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Kure Pollstergaard, Katrine","Wiingreen, Rikke","Gregersen, Rasmus","Mathiasen, Rene","Hansen, Bo Mølholm","Greisen, Gorm"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-409814"],"filters":{"added":"2023-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Educational level","Living arrangements","Preterm birth","Very low birth weight"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"socioeconomic outcomes beyond 000000000030 years of age in a cohort born with very low birth weight","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/apa.16557","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-409814","identifier":[{"id":"85139399832","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36176205","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10091976","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-1b799a5c-0984-4cf8-b4d4-0532235b05c1"],"keyword":["educational level","financial independence","living arrangements","preterm birth","very low birth weight"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DF704D51BAC817872F0C35777D19E00E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/apa.16557","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/334015046/Acta_Paediatrica_2022_Kure_Pollstergaard_Socioeconomic_outcomes_beyond_30_years_of_age_in_a_cohort_born_with_very_low.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Katrine","key":"kurepollstergaard