on technical procedures for simulation-based training in thoracic surgery : an international needs assessment","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-091.72-704336","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background: Previous studies have reported Blood type O to confer a lower risk of SARS-CoV-2 infection, while secretor status and other blood groups have been suspected to have a similar effect as well. Study design and methods: To determine whether any other blood groups influence testing positive for SARS-CoV-2, COVID-19 severity, or prolonged COVID-19, we used a large cohort of 650,156 Danish blood donors with varying available data for secretor status and blood groups ABO, Rh, Colton, Duffy, Diego, Dombrock, Kell, Kidd, Knops, Lewis, Lutheran, MNS, P1PK, Vel, and Yt. Of these, 36,068 tested positive for SARS-CoV-2 whereas 614,088 tested negative between 2020-02-17 and 2021-08-04. Associations between infection and blood groups were assessed using logistic regression models with sex and age as covariates. Results: The Lewis blood group antigen Le a displayed strongly reduced SARS-CoV-2 susceptibility OR 0.85 CI[0.79–0.93] p <.001. Compared to blood type O, the blood types B, A, and AB were found more susceptible toward infection with ORs 1.1 CI[1.06–1.14] p <.001, 1.17 CI[1.14–1.2] p <.001, and 1.2 CI[1.14–1.26] p <.001, respectively. No susceptibility associations were found for the other 13 blood groups investigated. There was no association between any blood groups and COVID-19 hospitalization or long COVID-19. No secretor status associations were found. Discussion: This study uncovers a new association to reduced SARS-CoV-2 susceptibility for Lewis type Le a and confirms the previous link to blood group O. The new association to Le a could be explained by a link between mucosal microbiome and SARS-CoV-2.","author_count":19,"contributors":["Moslemi, Camous","Sækmose, Susanne","Larsen, Rune","Brodersen, Thorsten","Didriksen, Maria","Hjalgrim, Henrik","Banasik, Karina","Nielsen, Kaspar René","Bruun, Mie T.","Dowsett, Joseph","Kasperen, Kathrine A.","Mikkelsen, Susan","Hansen, Thomas F.","Ullum, Henrik","Erikstrup, Christian","Olsson, Martin L.","Ostrowski, Sisse R.","Pedersen, Ole B."],"cuid":["S04-099.17-703916"],"filters":{"added":"2022-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Moslemi, Camous"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Lund University","Office for Medical Services","Region Skåne"],"keyword":["ABO","ABO Blood Group System","Blood Group Antigens","Blood Group Antigens genetics","Blood antigen","Blood groups","COVID 19 blood","COVID hospitalization","COVID severity","COVID susceptibility","Cohort Studies","Covid 19","Diego","Dombrock","Duffy","FUT2","FUT3","Genetic Predisposition to Disease","Humans","Kell","Kidd","Knops","Lewis","Long COVID 19","Lutheran","MNS","P1PK","Post Acute COVID 19 Syndrome","Rh","SARS CoV 2","Secretor","Vel","Yt","blood systems","long COVID symptoms"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a large cohort study of the effects of lewis abo 000000000013 other blood groups and secretor status on covid 000000000019 susceptibility severity and long covid 000000000019","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/trf.17170","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We thank the Danish Blood Donor Study for making data available for the research done in this publication. The collec