{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Functional seizures (FS) are episodes of paroxysmal involuntary movements and altered consciousness without the typical changes in the electroencephalography as with epilepsy. A multidisciplinary approach is the golden standard in the treatment of FS. This study examined the cross-sectoral collaboration and treatment modalities provided to children and adolescents after a diagnosis of FS. METHOD: A Danish nationwide cohort, consisting of 334 children and adolescents, aged 5-17 years, with a validated diagnosis of FS during the period 2004-2014 was studied. Medical record data were collected from diagnosing hospital departments. Management and treatment modalities from the time of diagnosis up to three months after diagnosis were explored. RESULTS: The most used treatment modalities were psychoeducation (n = 289, 86.5%) and follow-up in outpatient care (n = 192, 70.6%). A cross-sectoral collaboration was initiated for a third of cases (n = 98, 29.3%). The most commonly provided treatment combination consisted of psychoeducation, follow-up in outpatient care and psychotherapy; however, only a few patients received this specific combination (n = 14, 4.2%). CONCLUSIONS: The treatment applied was individualized and consisted of varying use of treatment modalities. Initiatives to curate clinical guidelines and implement a multidisciplinary treatment approach should be further explored to improve treatment for this young group of patients.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Sørensen, Nana Brandborg","Nielsen, René Ernst","Christensen, Ann-Eva","Kjeldsen, Mikkel K.","Rask, Charlotte Ulrikka","Christensen, Jakob","Hansen, Anne Sofie"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-691704"],"filters":{"added":"2023-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Sørensen, Nana Brandborg"],"keyword":["Danish nationwide cohort","Functional seizures","Management","Paediatric","Psychogenic nonepileptic seizures","Treatment"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"treatment provided in children and adolescents with functional seizures a danish nationwide cohort","src":["aau","au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/children10071218","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Jakob Christensen reports that financial support was provided by the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF16OC0019126). Jakob Christensen reports a relationship with Eisai Inc. that includes speaking and lecture fees. Jakob Christensen reports a relationship with UCB Inc. that includes speaking and lecture fees. René Ernst Nielsen has received funding for research or been an investigator for H. Lundbeck, Otsuka Pharmaceuticals, Compass, Boehringer Ingelheim and Janssen-Cilag. Furthermore, René Ernst Nielsen has received speakers fees from Bristol-Meyers Squibb, Astra Zeneca, Janssen-Cilag, Lundbeck, Servier, Otsuka Pharmaceuticals, Teva and Eli Lilly."],"id":"S02-100.00-691704","identifier":[{"id":"37508715","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10378534","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85169623805","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-2d8b3934-f549-4672-9f97-1c4789e8eb4a","au-f774488e-1350-4fd4-8473-bc1626da96e5"],"keyword":["Danish nationwide cohort","functional seizures","management","paediatric","psychogenic nonepileptic seizures","treatment"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-A34F86BC48291D99C08489B33B2857CB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/children10071218","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/545624969/S_rensen_et_al._2023_._Treatment_Provided_in_Children_and_Adolescents_with_Functional_Seizures_-_A_Danish_Nationwide_Cohort.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Psychiatry","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Health Science and Technology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Psychiatric Hospital for Children and Adolescents"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nana Brandborg","key":"sørensennanabrandborgpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"3b84af1b-7ba9-4a66-9b7e-100712f22429","name":"Sørensen, Nana Brandborg","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"René Ernst","key":"nielsenrenéernstpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"689bbd5c-f16d-4316-9717-29e68172bb57","name":"Nielsen, René Ernst","orcid":"0000-0002-7982-6352","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ann-Eva","key":"christensenannevapau","last":"Christensen","locid":"6b85a6be-dd8d-4396-ba07-8786573bae99","name":"Christensen, Ann-Eva","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mikkel Kristian","key":"kjeldsenmikkelkpau","last":"Kjeldsen","locid":"b111d61d-8611-4db9-9723-30fb769ce777","name":"Kjeldsen, Mikkel K.","name_org":"Kjeldsen, Mikkel Kristian","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Charlotte Ulrikka","key":"raskcharlotteulrikkapau","last":"Rask","locid":"1e308742-df61-47da-83ce-53d8a7c774c3","name":"Rask, Charlotte Ulrikka","orcid":"0000-0002-7426-0353","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jakob","key":"christensenjakobpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"e0bc29a0-3d22-4fa2-82f8-4d99059132c9","name":"Christensen, Jakob","orcid":"0000-0002-9385-6435","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne Sofie","key":"hansenannesofiepau","last":"Hansen","locid":"1a4148c3-ca6e-4d92-ac08-e981652a4177","name":"Hansen, Anne Sofie","orcid":"0000-0001-6101-2150","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37508715"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/children10071218","issn":["22279067"],"issue":"7","journal":"Children","paperid":"1218","publisher":"MDPI","status":"p","title":"Children","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85169623805"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-2d8b3934-f549-4672-9f97-1c4789e8eb4a","au-f774488e-1350-4fd4-8473-bc1626da96e5"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Treatment Provided in Children and Adolescents with Functional Seizures-A Danish Nationwide Cohort","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-691704","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Purpose The burden of different skin diseases may vary leading individuals to have different sensitivity to stress. Therefore, we compared the health-related quality of life (HRQoL) and stress before and during the universal stress from the severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus-2-pandemic in individuals with and without hyperhidrosis, hidradenitis suppurativa, or psoriasis. Methods The study cohort was the Danish Blood Donor Study. Overall, 12,798 participants completed a baseline questionnaire before the pandemic, in 2018–2019, and a follow-up questionnaire during the pandemic, in 2020. Regression determined the association between the skin diseases and outcomes. Outcomes were the physical and mental component summary (MCS, PCS, respectively), which assess the mental and physical HRQoL, and the perceived stress scale, which assesses stress in the past four weeks. Results Overall, 1168 (9.1%) participants had hyperhidrosis, 363 (2.8%) had hidradenitis suppurativa, and 402 (3.1%) had psoriasis. At follow-up, the participants with hyperhidrosis had worse MCS (coefficient −0.59 [95% confidence interval (CI) −1.05, −0.13]) and higher odds of moderate-to-severe stress (odds ratio 1.37 [95% CI 1.13, 1.65]) and the participants with hidradenitis suppurativa worse PCS (coefficient −0.74 [95% CI −1.21, −0.27]) than the control groups. The associations were independent of baseline HRQoL, stress, the Connor-Davidson Resilience scale, and other covariables. Psoriasis was not associated with the outcomes. Conclusion Individuals with hyperhidrosis or hidradenitis suppurativa experienced worse mental or physical well-being and individuals with hyperhidrosis also had higher stress during the pandemic compared to healthy individuals. This suggests that individuals with these skin diseases are particularly susceptible to external stress.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Henning, M. A. S.","Didriksen, M.","Ibler, K. S.","Ostrowski, S. R.","Erikstrup, C.","Nielsen, Kaspar René","Sækmose, S. G.","Hansen, T. F.","Ullum, H.","Thørner, L. W.","Kaspersen, K. A.","Mikkelsen, S.","Jemec, G. B. E.","Pedersen, O. B."],"cuid":["S03-099.93-715697"],"filters":{"added":"2023-07","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Henning, M. A. S."],"keyword":["Covid 19","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Hyperhidrosis","Psoriasis","Psychological","Quality of Life","Stress"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the differentiating effect of covid 000000000019 associated stress on the morbidity of blood donors with symptoms of hidradenitis suppurativa hyperhidrosis or psoriasis","src":["aau","au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/copyright/","type":"rem","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10239219/pdf/11136_2023_Article_3448.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-099.93-715697","identifier":[{"id":"37270451","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85161011185","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC10239219","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["aau-7a139dad-c863-4c0f-b871-97943e0c5e0c","au-8782ffd5-5544-4c3a-84e4-664cb1e15803","ku-550e7b5b-e18f-4505-b57e-60fe17f5998d"],"keyword":["COVID-19","Hidradenitis suppurativa","Hyperhidrosis","Psoriasis","Psychological","Quality of life","Stress"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-58C5052B5A810A15C7F18D54E5FD0F18"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/about/copyright/","