u"}],"pmid":["37270451"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s11136-023-03448-4","issn":["09629343"],"issue":"10","journal":"Quality of Life Research","pages":"2925-2937","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"Quality of Life Research","volume":"32"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85161011185"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-7a139dad-c863-4c0f-b871-97943e0c5e0c","au-8782ffd5-5544-4c3a-84e4-664cb1e15803","ku-550e7b5b-e18f-4505-b57e-60fe17f5998d"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The differentiating effect of COVID-19-associated stress on the morbidity of blood donors with symptoms of hidradenitis suppurativa, hyperhidrosis, or psoriasis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-099.93-715697","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Background. The etiology of central nervous system (CNS) tumors in children is largely unknown and population-based studies of genetic predisposition are lacking. Methods. In this prospective, population-based study, we performed germline whole-genome sequencing in 128 children with CNS tumors, supplemented by a systematic pedigree analysis covering 3543 close relatives. Results. Thirteen children (10%) harbored pathogenic variants in known cancer genes. These children were more likely to have medulloblastoma (OR 5.9, CI 1.6–21.2) and develop metasynchronous CNS tumors (P = 0.01). Similar carrier frequencies were seen among children with low-grade glioma (12.8%) and high-grade tumors (12.2%). Next, considering the high mortality of childhood CNS tumors throughout most of human evolution, we explored known pediatric-onset cancer genes, showing that they are more evolutionarily constrained than genes associated with risk of adult-onset malignancies (P = 5e−4) and all other genes (P = 5e−17). Based on this observation, we expanded our analysis to 2986 genes exhibiting high evolutionary constraint in 141,456 humans. This analysis identified eight directly causative loss-of-functions variants, and showed a dose-response association between degree of constraint and likelihood of pathogenicity—raising the question of the role of other highly constrained gene alterations detected. Conclusions. Approximately 10% of pediatric CNS tumors can be attributed to rare variants in known cancer genes. Genes associated with high risk of childhood cancer show evolutionary evidence of constraint.","author_count":16,"contributors":["Stoltze, Ulrik Kristoffer","Foss-Skiftesvik, Jon","van Overeem Hansen, Thomas","Byrjalsen, Anna","Sehested, Astrid","Scheie, David","Mikkelsen, Torben Stamm","Rasmussen, Simon","Bak, Mads","Okkels, Henrik","Callesen, Michael Thude","Skjøth-Rasmussen, Jane","Gerdes, Anne-Marie","Schmiegelow, Kjeld","Mathiasen, René","Wadt, Karin"],"cuid":["S04-099.53-681219"],"filters":{"added":"2022-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"corresponding_author":["Foss-Skiftesvik, Jon"],"keyword":["Adult","CNS tumors","Central Nervous System Neoplasms pathology","Cerebellar Neoplasms genetics","Child","Childhood cancer","Genetic Predisposition to Disease","Genomics","Glioma genetics","Humans","Predisposition","Prospective Studies","evolutionary constraint"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"genetic predisposition and evolutionary traces of pediatric cancer risk a prospective 000000000005 year population based genome sequencing study of children with cns tumors","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/neuonc/noac187","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S04-099.53-681219","identifier":[{"id":"85160264797","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000844276400001","type":"wos"},{"id":"35902210","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC10076945","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["aau-3344e068-fbd3-49b8-b092-52eaa61a7572","au-a48fb786-b53f-4a3f-845c-c8c46eb17355","ku-08475c4c-673a-4fbf-add1-10cdf35bb5e8","sdu-dd384395-8432-42fc-8fae-13025a461a93"],"keyword":["Adult","CNS tumors","Central Nervous System Neoplasms/pathology","Cerebellar Neoplasms/genetics","Child","Genetic Predisposition to Disease","Glioma/genetics","Humans","Prospective Studies","childhood cancer","evolutionary constraint","genomics","predisposition"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-7-38