{"abstract":"The emergence and dissemination of resistance to third- and fourth-generation cephalosporins among Enterobacteriaceae from different sources impose a global public health threat. Here, we characterized by whole-genome sequencing four Escherichia coli strains harboring the blaCTX–M–65 gene identified among 49 isolates from beef and pork collected at retail. The genomic content was determined using the Center for Genomic Epidemiology web tools. Additionally, the prediction and reconstruction of plasmids were conducted, the genetic platform of the blaCTX–M–65 genes was investigated, and phylogenetic analysis was carried out using 17 other genomes with the same sequence type and harboring the blaCTX–M–65 gene. All strains harbored blaCTX–M–65, blaOXA–1, and blaTEM–1B, and one also carried the blaSHV–12 gene. Other resistance genes, namely, qnrS2, aac(6′)-Ib-c, dfrA14, sul2, tetA, and mphA, were present in all the genomes; the mcr-1.1 gene was identified in the colistin-resistant strains. They belong to sequence type 2179, phylogenetic group B1, and serotype O9:H9 and carried plasmids IncI, IncFIC(FII), and IncFIB. All strains share an identical genetic environment with IS903 and ISEcp1 flanking the blaCTX–M–65 gene. It seems likely that the blaCTX–M–65 gene is located in the chromosome in all isolates based on deep in silico analysis. Our findings showed that the strains are clonally related and belong to two sub-lineages. This study reports the emergence of CTX-M-65-producing E. coli in Portugal in food products of animal origin. The chromosomal location of the blaCTX–M–65 gene may ensure a stable spread of resistance in the absence of selective pressure.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Leão, Célia","Clemente, Lurdes","Moura, Laura","Seyfarth, Anne Mette","Hansen, Inge M.","Hendriksen, Rene S.","Amaro, Ana"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-321345"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Amaro, Ana"],"country":["Portugal"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National Institute of Agrarian and Veterinary Research"],"keyword":["Chromosome","ESBL","Escherichia coli","Retail meat","WGS"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"emergence and clonal spread of ctx m 000000000065 producing i escherichia coli i from retail meat in portugal","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2021.653595","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We are grateful to all staff involved in the sampling and laboratory work for their technical support. Funding. This work was supported by the project PTDC/CVT-CVT/28469/2017 ?CIAinVET?Food-producing animals as reservoirs of resistance to Critically Important Antibiotics? financed by the ?Funda??o para a Ci?ncia e Tecnologia? (FCT), Portugal. CL was also funded by National Funds through FCT?Foundation for Science and Technology under the Project UIDB/05183/2020. This work was supported by the project PTDC/CVT-CVT/28469/2017 “CIAinVET—Food-producing animals as reservoirs of resistance to Critically Important Antibiotics” financed by the “Fundação para a Ciência e Tecnologia” (FCT), Portugal. CL was also funded by National Funds through FCT— Foundation for Science and Technology under the Project UIDB/05183/2020."],"id":"S01-000.00-321345","identifier":[{"id":"34354678","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85111918904","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8329498","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["dtu-386f17b7-1422-43c7-b5b8-33c606b81250"],"keyword":["CTX-M-65","Chromosome","ESBL","Escherichia coli","Retail meat","WGS"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-DA39464D00A487306CB65F123D992FEA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/262811347/fmicb_12_653595.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2021.653595","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"pt","key":"national institute of agrarian and veterinary research","loc":["Portugal","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"National Institute of Agrarian and Veterinary Research","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Food Institute","Technical University of Denmark, National Food Institute, Research Group for Genomic Epidemiology","Technical University of Denmark, National Food Institute, Research Group for Global Capacity Building"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Célia","key":"leãopau","last":"Leão","name":"Leão, Célia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lurdes","key":"clementepau","last":"Clemente","name":"Clemente, Lurdes","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Laura","key":"mourapau","last":"Moura","name":"Moura, Laura","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne Mette","key":"seyfarthpau","last":"Seyfarth","locid":"1e478afd-afd1-4256-abb2-3aed9340635b","name":"Seyfarth, Anne Mette","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Inge M.","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"89cde6dc-a8f7-4a0d-9309-24a248392250","name":"Hansen, Inge M.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rene S.","key":"hendriksenpau","last":"Hendriksen","locid":"d0695f89-50d1-4626-b4b2-0ea8773ccb3c","name":"Hendriksen, Rene S.","orcid":"0000-0003-2934-8214","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ana","key":"amaropau","last":"Amaro","name":"Amaro, Ana","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34354678"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fmicb.2021.653595","issn":["1664302X"],"journal":"Frontiers in Microbiology","paperid":"653595","publisher":"Frontiers Media SA","publisher_org":"Frontiers Media S.A.","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Microbiology","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85111918904"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-386f17b7-1422-43c7-b5b8-33c606b81250"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Emergence and Clonal Spread of CTX-M-65-Producing <i>Escherichia coli</i> From Retail Meat in Portugal","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-321345","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Domestication of horses fundamentally transformed long-range mobility and warfare1. However, modern domesticated breeds do not descend from the earliest domestic horse lineage associated with archaeological evidence of bridling, milking and corralling2–4 at Botai, Central Asia around 3500 bc3. Other longstanding candidate regions for horse domestication, such as Iberia5 and Anatolia6, have also recently been challenged. Thus, the genetic, geographic and temporal origins of modern domestic horses have remained unknown. Here we pinpoint the Western Eurasian steppes, especially the lower Volga-Don region, as the homeland of modern domestic horses. Furthermore, we map the population changes accompanying domestication from 273 ancient horse genomes. This reveals that modern domestic horses ultimately replaced almost all other local populations as they expanded rapidly across Eurasia from about 2000 bc, synchronously with equestrian material culture, including Sintashta spoke-wheeled chariots. We find that equestrianism involved strong selection for critical locomotor and behavioural adaptations at the GSDMC and ZFPM1 genes. Our results reject the commonly held association7 between horseback riding and the massive expansion of Yamnaya steppe pastoralists into Europe around 3000 bc8,9 driving the spread of Indo-European languages10. This contrasts with the scenario in Asia where Indo-Iranian languages, chariots and horses spread together, following the early second millennium bc Sintashta culture11,12.","author_count":163,"contributors":["Librado, Pablo","Khan, Naveed","Fages, Antoine","Kusliy, Mariya A.","Suchan, Tomasz","Tonasso-Calvière, Laure","Schiavinato, Stéphanie","Alioglu, Duha","Fromentier, Aurore","Perdereau, Aude","Aury, Jean-Marc","Gaunitz, Charleen","Chauvey, Lorelei","Seguin-Orlando, Andaine","Der Sarkissian, Clio","Southon, John","Shapiro, Beth","Tishkin, Alexey A.","Kovalev, Alexey A.","Alquraishi, Saleh","Alfarhan, Ahmed H.","Al-Rasheid, Khal