{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: To test the hypothesis that patient-initiated follow up reduces the fear of cancer recurrence (FCR) and healthcare use when compared with traditional hospital-based follow up. DESIGN: Pragmatic, multicentre randomised trial. SETTING: Four Danish departments of gynaecology between May 2013 and May 2016. POPULATION: One hundred and fifty-six women diagnosed with International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) stage I low-intermediate risk endometrial carcinoma. METHODS: Women allocated to the control group attended hospital-based follow up consisting of regular outpatient visits for 3 years after primary treatment. Women in the intervention group were instructed in patient-initiated follow up, which included careful instruction in alarm symptoms and options for self-referral rather than a schedule of examinations. MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES: The primary end point was FCR as measured by the Fear of Cancer Recurrence Inventory (FCRI) after 10 months of follow up. Secondary end points included cancer-related use of primary and secondary health care during the first 10 months after treatment. RESULTS: In the primary analysis, FCR decreased significantly more in the control group from baseline to 10 months of follow up (difference -5.9, 95% CI -10.9 to -0.9). The majority of this improvement happened after only 3 months of follow up. Women receiving the intervention had fewer examinations at the department compared with the control group (0 versus 2 median visits, P < 0.01) and 58% of these examinations were scheduled because of vaginal bleeding. CONCLUSIONS: Hospital-based follow up alleviates FCR significantly more than patient-initiated follow up, though the estimated difference was small. Patient-initiated follow up is a feasible, potentially cost-reducing follow-up approach in a population of endometrial cancer survivors with low risk of recurrence. The decision to use patient-initiated follow up should balance these benefits and harms. TWEETABLE ABSTRACT: Patient-initiated follow up reduces healthcare use but maintains fear of recurrence in endometrial cancer. PLAIN LANGUAGE SUMMARY: Why and how was the study carried out? Follow up of women with endometrial cancer is resource consuming and previous research suggests that it is not effective. Even though the women benefit from reassurance at follow up, routine examinations may also remind the women of the disease and induce fear of cancer recurrence. Furthermore, routine follow up may delay recurrence diagnosis, because the women do not report their symptoms until the next scheduled visit. In the research explained in this article, patient-initiated follow up was evaluated as an alternative to traditional follow up. The women were randomly assigned to one of two follow-up programmes: regular gynaecological examinations at the department of gynaecology or self-referral with careful instruction in alarm symptoms, that is, patient-initiated follow up. The level of fear of cancer recurrence in the two groups was obtained by questionnaires. Information on healthcare use was obtained by questionnaires and a chart review. What were the main findings? Regular examinations at the department of gynaecology reduced the fear of cancer recurrence significantly more than patient-initiated follow up, though the difference was small. Women who were instructed in alarm symptoms, under self-referral, were able to monitor their symptoms, and this approach significantly reduced the number of examinations at the department of gynaecology. What are the limitations of the work? Participants in the self-referral group knew that they were examined less than other women, and this may have induced fear of cancer recurrence. Similarly, the regular completion of questionnaires regarding fear of cancer recurrence may have reminded the women of the disease and diminished the difference between the two groups. What are the implications for patients Patient-initiated follow up reduced healthcare use but maintained fear of cancer recurrence in women who had survived early-stage endometrial cancer. Future analyses on quality of life and cost-effectiveness are needed to balance the benefits and harms of patient-initiated follow up.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Jeppesen, Mette Moustgaard","Jensen, Pernille Tine","Hansen, Dorte Gilså","Christensen, René dePont","Mogensen, Ole"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-629376"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Jeppesen, Mette Moustgaard"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karolinska Institute"],"keyword":["Aged","Alarm symptoms","Appointments and Schedules","Cancer","Endometrial Neoplasms psychology","Endometrial carcinoma","Fear","Female","Follow Up Studies","Gynaecological malignancy","Humans","Middle Aged","Neoplasm Recurrence Local psychology","Patient Participation","Population Surveillance methods","Post treatment surveillance","Recurrence","Uterus","patient initiated follow up"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"patient initiated follow up affects fear of recurrence and healthcare use a randomised trial in early stage endometrial cancer","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/143378489/PA_L81FZ.PDF","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-629376","identifier":[{"id":"85052635460","type":"scopus"},{"id":"29978593","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-c419b597-f867-4352-ae13-bf995bc88a91"],"keyword":["Aftercare/psychology","Aged","Alarm symptoms","Appointments and Schedules","Carcinoma/psychology","Endometrial Neoplasms/psychology","Fear","Female","Follow-Up Studies","Humans","Middle Aged","Neoplasm Recurrence, Local/psychology","Patient Participation","Population Surveillance/methods","cancer","endometrial carcinoma","gynaecological malignancy","patient-initiated follow up","post-treatment surveillance","recurrence","uterus"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-25286F128273D75F65625687E34F86B9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/143378489/PA_L81FZ.PDF","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Gynaecology and Obstetrics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"karolinska institute","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska Institute","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"M. M.","key":"jeppesenpau","last":"Jeppesen","locid":"79c59d83-9d4b-4abd-acf5-7085b6c42ffd","name":"Jeppesen, Mette Moustgaard","name_org":"Jeppesen, M. M.","orcid":"0000-0001-7390-5167","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"P. T.","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"ef83b3df-03b5-4eea-9e38-b8ca5467e677","name":"Jensen, Pernille Tine","name_org":"Jensen, P. T.","orcid":"0000-0002-1637-6040","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"D. G.","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"a2826791-eb97-4bca-8af7-448b0940e6e5","name":"Hansen, Dorte Gilså","name_org":"Hansen, D. G.","orcid":"0000-0002-5946-9968","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"R. D.","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"3f33c4f0-abcb-42c6-85af-2d24bcf025ff","name":"Christensen, René dePont","name_org":"Christensen, R. D.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"O.","key":"mogensenpau","last":"Mogensen","locid":"ddd33772-ea7a-4f39-87ee-f590fa739d37","name":"Mogensen, Ole","name_org":"Mogensen, O.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29978593"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/1471-0528.15396","issn":["01407686","14700328"],"issn_org":["14700328"],"issue":"13","journal":"BJOG: An International Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology","pages":"1705-1714","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"BJOG: An International Journal of Obstetrics and Gynaecology","volume":"125"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85052635460"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-c419b597-f867-4352-ae13-bf995bc88a91"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Patient-initiated follow up affects fear of recurrence and healthcare use : a randomised trial in early-stage endometrial cancer","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-629376","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Objective To study the risk of a second malignant neoplasm (SMN) and mortality in patients with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) with a history of a primary cancer diagnosis and treated with biological disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (bDMARD). Methods Among patients with RA (n=15 286) registered in the DANBIO Register during 2000-2011, 1678 had a primary cancer according to the Danish Cancer Registry. HRs for SMN and death were calculated. Results During follow-up there were 279 patients with RA contributing person-years to the bDMARDs use before their primary cancer diagnosis, 220 to the only after, 92 to the both before and after, while 1203 patients with RA contributed to the non-use strata. Ever use of bDMARDs was associated with a HR of 1.11 (95% CI 0.74 to 1.67) for developing a SMN compared with non-use (cancer site adjusted). The HR for death associated with bDMARD use before the primary cancer diagnosis was increased 1.53 (95% CI 1.13 to 2.09). After further adjustment for extent of the primary cancer, the HR for death was 1.20 (95% CI 0.88 to 1.63) for bDMARDs use before cancer, 1.36 (95% CI 0.78 to 2.39) for bDMARD use only after cancer and 1.22 (95% CI 0.70 to 2.13) for use both before and after the cancer. Conclusions Among patients with RA with a history of cancer, treatment with bDMARDs was not associated with increased risk of SMN. No clear conclusion can be drawn regarding mortality in bDMARD-treated patients with RA.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Dreyer, Lene","Cordtz, René L.","Hansen, Inger Marie J.","Kristensen, Lars Erik","Hetland, Merete Lund","Mellemkjaer, Lene"],"cuid":["S02-099.94-047604"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Aged 80 and over","Antirheumatic Agents adverse effects","Arthritis","Arthritis Rheumatoid complications","Biological Products adverse effects","Cohort Studies","DMARDs biologic","Denmark","Epidemiology","Female","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Middle Aged","Neoplasms Second Primary chemically induced","Neoplasms mortality","Proportional Hazards Models","Registries","Time Factors","Young Adult","anti TNF"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"risk of second malignant neoplasm and mortality in patients with rheumatoid arthritis treated with biological dmards a danish population based cohort study","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85044817699","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-099.94-047604","identifier":[{"id":"29217620","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85044817699","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-fad16703-b37d-4c4f-8a17-19474b00d7cb","sdu-fcd80da3-77f6-436c-9930-f5ae254c98a5"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Aged, 80 and over","Antirheumatic Agents/adverse effects","Arthritis, Rheumatoid/complications","Biological Products/adverse effects","Cohort Studies","DMARDs (biologic)","Denmark","Female","Humans","Journal Article","Male","Middle Aged","Neoplasms, Second Primary/chemically induced","Neoplasms/mortality","Proportional Hazards Models","Registries","Time Factors","Young Adult","anti-TNF","arthritis","epidemiology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-05B0B49F61C2C4331FC0ED4FED19D0B6"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Bispebjerg and Frederiksberg Hospitals","Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Medicine (Svendborg)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, Medical Department"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"dcrc danish cancer society research center","l2":"DI-NP","l2n":"Non-Profit Organisations","l3":"DI-NP-CAN","l3nn":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DCRC Danish Cancer Society Research Center","namevar":["Kræftens Bekæmpelse"],"nora_code":"DI-NP-CAN","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","first":"Lene","key":"dreyerpau","last":"Dreyer","locid":"d9f655af-c3aa-4ec1-bfd7-c61f0cd51219","name":"Dreyer, Lene","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"René L","key":"cordtzpau","last":"Cordtz","name":"Cordtz, René L","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Inger Marie J","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"e6350066-c71d-48a0-945e-259b7989d1c6","name":"Jensen Hansen, Inger Marie","name_org":"Hansen, Inger Marie J","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Lars Erik","key":"kristensenpau","last":"Kristensen","locid":"abb37858-fdb4-4151-a6d2-8b0d5b81141c","name":"Kristensen, Lars Erik","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Merete L.","key":"hetlandpau","last":"Hetland","locid":"3407b4a7-174d-43db-bae6-74e7f035a81f","name":"Hetland, Merete Lund","name_org":"Hetland, Merete L.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"mellemkjaerpau","last":"Mellemkjaer","name":"Mellemkjaer, Lene","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29217620"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1136/annrheumdis-2017-212086","issn":["00034967","14682060"],"issn_org":["00034967"],"issue":"4","journal":"Annals of the Rheumatic Diseases","pages":"510-514","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Annals of the Rheumatic Diseases","volume":"77"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85044817699"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Non-Profit Organisations","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Non-Profit Organisations","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-fad16703-b37d-4c4f-8a17-19474b00d7cb","sdu-fcd80da3-77f6-436c-9930-f5ae254c98a5"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Risk of second malignant neoplasm and mortality in patients with rheumatoid arthritis treated with biological DMARDs : A Danish population-based cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.94-047604","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Tumour budding (TB) and Tumour Stroma Ratio (TSR) may be rewarding in the treatment stratification of patients with stage II colon cancer. However, lack of standardization may exclude these parameters from being used in a clinical setting. The purpose of this methodologic study was to compare stereology with semi-quantitative estimations of TSR, to investigate the intra-tumoural heterogeneity of TB and TSR, and to assess the intra- and inter-observer agreement. METHODS: Three paraffin embedded tumour blocks, one of them representing the deepest invasive front, were selected from each of 43 patients treated for stage II colon cancer. TSR was estimated in H&E sections semi-quantitatively using conventional microscopy, and stereologically on scanned slides, using the newCAST stereology platform. TB was scored across 10 high power fields at the invasive front in cytokeratin AE1/AE3 stained sections. RESULTS: Subjective, semi-quantitative estimates of TSR significantly correlated to the stereological estimates, with the best correlation found for sections with the deepest invasive tumour penetration (σ = 0.621, p < 0.001). Inter-observer agreement was moderate to substantial for both TB (Κappa = 0.46-0.73) and TSR (Κappa = 0.70-0.75). The Intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) for TSR varied from 0.322 based on stereological hotspot estimation to 0.648 for the semi-quantitative evaluation. For TB, ICC varied from 0.646 based on continuous data to 0.698 based on categorical data (cut-off: 10 buds). Thus, the intra-tumoural heterogeneity for both TB and the semi-quantitative estimation of TSR was low. CONCLUSION: We recommend using only one tissue section representing the deepest invasive tumour area for estimation of TSR. For TB we recommend using one tissue section; however due to low representation of high-budding tumours, results must be considered with caution.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Eriksen, Ann C","Andersen, Johnnie Bremholm","Lindebjerg, Jan","dePont Christensen, René","Hansen, Torben F","Kjær-Frifeldt, Sanne","Sørensen, Flemming Brandt"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-015567"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Eriksen, Ann Christina"],"keyword":["Adenocarcinoma diagnosis","Adult","Aged","Colon cancer","Colonic Neoplasms diagnosis","Colorectal Neoplasms diagnosis","Colorectal cancer","Female","Heterogeneity","High risk","Humans","INDEX","Immunohistochemistry methods","Male","Microenvironment","Middle Aged","Predict","Prognosis","Proportion","Reproducibility","Soft Tissue Neoplasms diagnosis","Survival","Tumour budding","Tumour stroma ratio","Validation","stage II"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"does heterogeneity matter in the estimation of tumour budding and tumour stroma ratio in colon cancer","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13000-018-0697-9","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-015567","identifier":[{"id":"29558947","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85044196420","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000427894300001","type":"wos"},{"id":"PMC5859415","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-28d07cbc-8726-4c6d-8fcd-e8e1cb94d967","sdu-f6318572-b9cc-4a82-bd67-ba6a353f2a73"],"keyword":["Adenocarcinoma/diagnosis","Adult","Aged","COLORECTAL-CANCER","Colon cancer","Colonic Neoplasms/diagnosis","Colorectal Neoplasms/diagnosis","Female","HIGH-RISK","Heterogeneity","Humans","INDEX","Immunohistochemistry/methods","MICROENVIRONMENT","Male","Middle Aged","PREDICT","PROPORTION","Prognosis","REPRODUCIBILITY","STAGE-II","SURVIVAL","Soft Tissue Neoplasms/diagnosis","Tumour budding","Tumour stroma ratio","VALIDATION"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-9F672DBAFA839026286EE76AF9915FFA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/141402370/Does_heterogeneity_matter_in_the_estimation_of_tumour_budding_and_tumour_stroma_ratio_in_colon_cancer.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13000-018-0697-9","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Lillebælt Hospital","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Lillebælt Hospital, Research Unit of Clinical Pathology (Vejle)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Core Centre for Molecular Morphology, Section for Stereology and Microscopy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Visiopharm A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ann C.","key":"eriksenpau","last":"Eriksen","locid":"c7b52d9f-48c6-46ce-921f-ac56c8e8d22e","name":"Eriksen, Ann Christina","name_org":"Eriksen, Ann C.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Johnnie B","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"01a3967f-ee5f-42b5-9d7b-1f0d25fab2b8","name":"Andersen, Johnnie Bremholm","name_org":"Andersen, Johnnie B","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan","key":"lindebjergpau","last":"Lindebjerg","locid":"5de10dbb-de45-4c38-8ed2-35bd614d5459","name":"Lindebjerg, Jan","orcid":"0000-0002-2055-4241","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"René","key":"depontchristensenpau","last":"dePont Christensen","locid":"3f33c4f0-abcb-42c6-85af-2d24bcf025ff","name":"Christensen, René dePont","name_org":"dePont Christensen, René","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Torben F.","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"72d28643-c440-4bbd-ad2b-eef0903a9438","name":"Hansen, Torben Frøstrup","name_org":"Hansen, Torben F.","orcid":"0000-0001-7476-671X","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sanne","key":"kjærfrifeldtpau","last":"Kjær-Frifeldt","locid":"6fdb727e-ac1e-4c28-b933-5510959bbc92","name":"Kjær-Frifeldt, Sanne","orcid":"0009-0009-9594-5042","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Flemming B","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"db856600-d106-4b0a-83f1-d7245f5249bd","name":"Sørensen, Flemming Brandt","name_org":"Sørensen, Flemming B","orcid":"0000-0002-5446-1035","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29558947"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s13000-018-0697-9","issn":["17461596"],"issue":"1","journal":"Diagnostic Pathology","pages":"20","paperid":"20","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"Diagnostic Pathology","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85044196420"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Private Research","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Other Companies","Private Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark","visiopharm a/s"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-28d07cbc-8726-4c6d-8fcd-e8e1cb94d967","sdu-f6318572-b9cc-4a82-bd67-ba6a353f2a73"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Does heterogeneity matter in the estimation of tumour budding and tumour stroma ratio in colon cancer?","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-015567","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: High-risk patients with stage II colon cancer (CC) may benefit from adjuvant chemotherapy, but additional prognostic markers are needed for better stratification. We investigated the prognostic value of tumour stroma ratio (TSR) and tumour budding (TB). METHODS: A nationwide population-based cohort of 573 patients with stage II CC was included. TSR was scored on hematoxylin and eosin sections as low TSR (> 50% stroma) and high TSR (≤ 50% stroma). TB was evaluated in hotspots on pan-cytokeratin stained sections in 10 high power fields (HPF) at the invasive front and classified by the mean number of buds per HPF as high grade budding (≥ 10 buds) or low-grade budding (< 10 buds). The prognostic value was investigated in Cox proportional hazard models for recurrence-free survival (RFS) and overall survival (OS). RESULTS: Low TSR was associated with worse RFS (HR = 1.342 (95% CI 1.006-1.791), p = 0.045) and OS (HR = 1.376 (95% CI 1.016-1.862), p = 0.039). Furthermore, an association was found between low TSR and microsatellite stabile tumours (p < 0.001). The mean number of buds per HPF was associated to TSR with increasing number of buds related to a lower TSR (p = 0.026). No statistically significant prognostic impact of TB regarding OS or RFS was detected. CONCLUSIONS: TSR provided valuable prognostic information, and adding TSR to the current risk stratification may contribute to better patient selection. The estimates of TSR and TB were found to be associated, but no prognostic value of TB was documented.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Eriksen, Ann Christina","Sørensen, Flemming Brandt","Lindebjerg, Jan","Hager, Henrik","dePont Christensen, René","Kjær-Frifeldt, Sanne","Hansen, Torben F"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-030461"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Eriksen, Ann Christina"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Child","Child Preschool","Colon cancer stage II","Colonic Neoplasms pathology","Denmark","Female","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Neoplasm Recurrence Local","Neoplasm Staging","Prognosis","Prognostic markers","Retrospective Studies","Survival","Tumour budding","Tumour stroma ratio"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-OTH","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-OTH","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the prognostic value of tumour stroma ratio and tumour budding in stage ii colon cancer a nationwide population based study","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00384-018-3076-9","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-030461","identifier":[{"id":"85047194393","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC6060800","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"29785462","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-f6cc948b-7918-49b9-b8e7-a33050d5c4a2","sdu-42c859db-139a-4511-8a64-9cf0942723c5"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Child","Child, Preschool","Colon cancer stage II","Colonic Neoplasms/pathology","Denmark","Female","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Neoplasm Recurrence, Local","Neoplasm Staging","Prognosis","Prognostic markers","Retrospective Studies","Survival","Tumour budding","Tumour stroma ratio"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-8299D046A5A7B5DB1A1D12149316E92B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/142969016/The_prognostic_value_of_tumour_stroma_ratio_and_tumour_budding_in_stage_II.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/166728857/Eriksen2018_Article_ThePrognosticValueOfTumourStro.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{