-1124","publisher":"Springer Nature","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Colorectal Disease","volume":"33"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85047194393"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Other Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark","danish colorectal cancer group"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-f6cc948b-7918-49b9-b8e7-a33050d5c4a2","sdu-42c859db-139a-4511-8a64-9cf0942723c5"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The prognostic value of tumour stroma ratio and tumour budding in stage II colon cancer. A nationwide population-based study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-030461","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"This study compares the effects of team-sport training, for sedentary men with lifestyle diseases, with fitness training in a pragmatic set-up in a community health centre (CHC). Thirty-two men in the fitness group (FiG) and 36 men in the team-sport group (TsG) completed the training and trained for 60-90 min, two times/week for 12-16 weeks. In FiG and TsG, mean heart rate (HR) during training was 73.2% and 74.5% of HRmax, respectively. Percentage of training time above 90%HRmax was 6 ± 9% and 10 ± 15% and the percentage of participants who spent > 10% of total training time with HR > 90%HRmax was 20% and 41%, in FiG and TsG, respectively. In FiG, total fat mass was reduced by 3.5%  (P < 0.01), while performance in the 6 min walking test (6MWT) increased by 11%  (P < 0.001). In TsG, total fat mass was reduced by 2.2%  (P < 0.01), while 6MWT performance improved by 5%  (P < 0.05). Between-group differences were observed for systolic BP (P = 0.041) and mean arterial pressure (P = 0.050) in favour of TsG and for sit-to-stand test (P = 0.031) in favour of FiG. In conclusion, small-sided team sport is a worthy alternative to fitness training since the overall health effects are comparable, for example, improved balance and reduced fat mass. Team sport elicits high heart rates and improves cardiovascular health by reducing blood pressure, while fitness training improves sit-to-stand test performance related to activity of daily living.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Møller, Trine Kjeldgaard Tang","Nielsen, Tina-Thea","Andersen, Rene","Lundager, I","Hansen, Henriette Folkmann","Ottesen, Laila","Krustrup, Peter","Randers, Morten B"],"cuid":["S03-090.67-038820"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Randers, Morten Bredsgaard"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Exeter"],"keyword":["Adiposity","Adult","Blood Pressure","Exercise","Exercise Therapy","Heart Rate","Humans","Male","Physical Fitness","Time Factors"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"OT":["Københavns Kommune"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-KP"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-KP"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"health effects of 000000000012 weeks of team sport training and fitness training in a community health centre for sedentary men with lifestyle diseases","src":["kp","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-KP"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/2018/1571807","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The authors would like to acknowledge the efforts of the many participants and the close collaboration with the staff of prevention centre Nørrebro, in particular Birgitte Gade Kofoed, Sten Skafte, and Lars Bjørnø as well as Anna Bachmann Boye and Anne Kjærgaard Svendsen from the Municipality of Copenhagen. The study was supported by the Municipality of Copenhagen, Helsefonden and Nordea-fonden."],"id":"S03-090.67-038820","identifier":[{"id":"85047872877","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC5977017","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"29888250","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["kp-4bc7c345-9256-447b-8226-1866935bd1e2","ku-43b6b79b-a742-401e-b9dc-23a19292c12f","sdu-b2144da8-b1bc-4605-a378-5731b2da0258"],"keyword":["Adiposity","Adult","Blood Pressure","Exercise","Exercise Therapy","Heart Rate","Humans","Male","Physical Fitness","Time Factors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-135B2A0B7A08B573FE5FBD8C65392147"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/197734248/M_ller_et_al_BioMed_Research_International_2018_Vol_2018_e1